</dialog>

<section class="intro" id="about">
  <div class="intro__wrap">
    <p class="intro__lead">
      Anna Atkins laid each specimen of seaweed flat upon paper soaked in iron salts,
      placed a sheet of glass over it, and let the sun print its shadow.
    </p>
    <div class="intro__body">
      <p>
        The process — <em>cyanotype</em>, invented in 1842 by her friend Sir John Herschel —
        renders everything it touches in two notes: <strong>Prussian blue</strong>, and the
        white ghost of the plant. There was no negative, no enlargement, no darkroom; only
        sun, water, and the algae themselves, pressed to paper one by one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Between October 1843 and 1853 she produced a complete photographic flora of the
        British algae in three volumes, with hand-lettered titles printed by the same
        cyanotype process. About a dozen sets survive. This page reproduces the New York
        Public Library’s copy — Sir John Herschel’s own — and pairs each plate with the
        species it depicts, its habitat, the words handwritten on the print, and, where
        possible, a modern photograph of the same alga taken in the wild.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<nav class="key" aria-label="Filter by group">
